WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.167
Before we talk about modules and classes, let us first ask why organization matters.

00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:10.492
When a program is very small, one file can feel simple.

00:00:10.492 --> 00:00:15.982
You might have a few variables, a loop, maybe a function, and a print statement.

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:21.177
That is manageable. But real analytics work often grows quickly.

00:00:21.177 --> 00:00:25.982
You may need to clean data, calculate metrics, create charts, run checks, and repeat parts of the process.

00:00:25.982 --> 00:00:32.847
If all of that logic is placed into one long script, the file becomes difficult to understand.

00:00:32.847 --> 00:00:35.772
Organized code gives related work a clear home.

00:00:35.772 --> 00:00:43.312
For example, cleaning functions could go into a file called cleaning.py.

00:00:43.312 --> 00:00:44.987
Metric calculations could go into metrics.py.

00:00:44.987 --> 00:00:48.492
Chart functions could go into charts.py.

00:00:48.492 --> 00:00:51.597
Then a main file can use those tools without becoming too long.

00:00:51.597 --> 00:00:54.657
This also helps future you.

00:00:54.657 --> 00:01:02.152
A few weeks later, when you return to the project, organized code makes it easier to remember where

00:01:02.152 --> 00:01:05.592
things are. It also helps teams.

00:01:05.592 --> 00:01:08.117
If another person reads your work, clear file names, function names,

00:01:08.117 --> 00:01:11.957
and class names make the project easier to review.

00:01:11.957 --> 00:01:16.537
The main idea is simple: organization reduces confusion.

00:01:16.537 --> 00:01:20.640
It helps code become easier to read, test, update, and reuse.
